    {
     "type": "practice",
     "title": "Practice: Find Your Window's Edges",
     "content": "For the next week, log moments when you tip out of your window of tolerance. Above (panic, rage, racing): note the time, the trigger, the body state, and how long until you came back. Below (numb, far away, fog): same notes. Don't try to change anything yet — just collect data.\n\nBy day seven, you will have a rough map of your window's current width and which way you tend to fall first. Many people discover that they tip into dissociation more than into panic, or vice versa — and that one direction is more familiar than they realized. Knowing the direction is half the work. The other half — widening the window — is what every body practice in lesson 7 will help you do.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Practice: Find Your Edges</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">A week of window data</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"940\" height=\"320\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.15)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Above the Window</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"305\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Time? Trigger?</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"345\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Body state at peak?</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"405\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">How long until return?</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"465\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\">Panic, rage, racing heart.</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"540\" width=\"940\" height=\"340\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.15)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"600\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Below the Window</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"660\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Time? Trigger?</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"700\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Body state at trough?</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"740\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">How long until return?</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"800\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Numb, distant, foggy.</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Map the edges before you try to move them."
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "practice",
     "title": "Practice: A Conversation With One Part",
     "content": "Find ten quiet minutes. Imagine the part of you that has the loudest opinion this week — the angry part, the perfectionist part, the part that wants to numb out, the part that withdraws. Don't argue with it. Sit beside it.\n\nAsk it three questions, in this order. First: 'What are you trying to protect?' Listen, write down whatever comes. Second: 'How old are you?' Notice the answer; often it surprises. Third: 'What do you need from me right now?' Listen. Write the answer down.\n\nThis is a first taste of parts work. You are not creating multiple personalities; you are practicing the IFS stance of curiosity toward your own internal cast. Many people find that ten minutes of this softens an inner critic that they have been fighting for years. You cannot integrate parts you have not spoken to. Start the conversation.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Practice: Talk to One Part</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Three questions, ten minutes</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"940\" height=\"340\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.15)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The Three Questions</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"310\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">1. What are you trying to protect?</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"360\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">2. How old are you?</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"410\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">3. What do you need from me now?</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"480\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\">Don't argue. Sit beside.</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"560\" width=\"940\" height=\"320\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"620\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">What to Notice</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"690\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">The age often surprises.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"730\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Protection motive softens conflict.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"790\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Write the answers down.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"830\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Curiosity is the medicine.</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Curiosity, not combat, with the parts of you."
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "quiz",
     "question": "Within the window-of-tolerance model, what does it mean to be 'above' the window vs 'below' it, and what's the goal of trauma practice?",
     "options": [
      {
       "text": "Above = hyperarousal (panic, rage); below = hypoarousal (numbness, shutdown). The goal is to widen the window so more arousal stays integrated",
       "correct": true
      },
      {
       "text": "Above and below are equivalent psychological terms",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "The goal of trauma practice is to live permanently above the window",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "The window does not exist for trauma survivors",
       "correct": false
      }
     ],
     "explanation": "Above the window is sympathetic over-activation — panic, rage, flashback. Below is dorsal vagal shutdown — numbness, dissociation. Trauma narrows the window so small triggers tip a person out. Body-based practice, parts work, and good therapy gradually widen the window so larger swings can be held without dissociation or panic.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Quiz: Window of Tolerance</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Widen the middle</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"940\" height=\"360\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.15)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Above vs Below</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"310\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Above: panic, rage, racing.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"350\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Below: numb, far, foggy.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"410\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Inside: connected and alive.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"470\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\">Trauma narrows the middle; practice widens it.</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"580\" width=\"940\" height=\"280\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"640\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The Goal</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"710\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">More arousal stays integrated.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"750\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Fewer trips above or below.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"810\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">More of life lived in the middle.</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Widen the room; live more of life inside it."
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "quote",
     "title": "Selves That Hold Each Other",
     "content": "Closing thought from van der Kolk:\n\n*'The body holds the score, and the self holds the body together. When the self has had to split to survive, the work of healing is to coax the pieces back into a room where they can finally see each other.'*\n\nThe room is built slowly. Every part of you that ever did the work of survival earns the right to a hearing. The next four lessons turn from theory to therapy — EMDR, body practices, neurofeedback, and finally community and meaning. Each is an attempt to build that room, by a different door.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Selves That Hold Each Other</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Coaxing the pieces back</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"200\" width=\"940\" height=\"500\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"320\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">\"The body holds the score,</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"360\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">and the self holds the body together.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"425\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">When the self has had to split</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"460\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">to survive, the work of healing</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"495\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">is to coax the pieces back.\"</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"610\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\">— van der Kolk on integration</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"740\" width=\"940\" height=\"310\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.15)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"800\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Coming next</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"870\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Four therapies, four doors:</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"905\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">EMDR, body, neurofeedback, community.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"965\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Each builds the room a different way.</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Healing is hospitality between the parts of a self."
     }
    }
