    {
     "type": "visual",
     "title": "Three Polyvagal States, Three Strategies",
     "content": "Polyvagal-informed therapy gives clinician and patient a simple, accurate map of where the nervous system is right now — and what intervention fits.\n\n**Ventral vagal (green):** social engagement online. Strategy: connect, learn, integrate.\n**Sympathetic (yellow):** mobilization, anxiety, anger. Strategy: discharge the activation — movement, walks, vigorous breath, then return to green.\n**Dorsal vagal (red):** shutdown, freeze, numb. Strategy: very gentle reactivation — slow movement, voice, soft contact, NOT vigorous breath or exposure (which can deepen shutdown).\n\nThe critical clinical insight: **a person in dorsal vagal shutdown should not be treated as if they were in sympathetic activation**. The interventions are nearly opposite. Many failed therapies fail by mismatching state and strategy. Polyvagal-informed practice teaches everyone in the room — clinician and patient — to read the state first.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Three States, Three Strategies</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Match the intervention to the state</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"940\" height=\"230\" rx=\"15\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"235\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Green — ventral vagal</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"305\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Connection online</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"345\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Strategy: integrate, learn,</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"385\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">do trauma processing here.</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"430\" width=\"940\" height=\"230\" rx=\"15\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.15)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"485\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Yellow — sympathetic</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"555\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Mobilization, anxious, angry</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"595\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Strategy: discharge — walk,</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"635\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">shake, vigorous breath, then green.</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"680\" width=\"940\" height=\"230\" rx=\"15\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.15)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"735\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Red — dorsal vagal</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"805\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Shutdown, numb, far away</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"845\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Strategy: very gentle reactivation,</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"885\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">voice, soft movement; not exposure.</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"930\" width=\"940\" height=\"120\" rx=\"15\" fill=\"rgba(239,68,68,0.15)\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"1000\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mismatching state and strategy harms.</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Read the state before you choose the technique."
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Why Yoga Specifically?",
     "content": "Many forms of movement help trauma. Why does van der Kolk single out yoga for the most rigorous trial work?\n\nThree features:\n\n**Coupled breath and movement:** every pose is paired with a deliberate breath. This trains conscious co-regulation of two systems that trauma decouples.\n\n**Interoceptive demand:** yoga asks the practitioner to notice — Where is my foot? My breath? My shoulder? — directly retraining the dysregulated insula.\n\n**Slow, safe approach to the body:** survivors who avoid the body for years find yoga's invitation gradual enough to accept. The carpet, the props, the predictable sequence remove threat.\n\nThis does not mean yoga is the only or always the best body practice. Tai chi, qigong, dance, swimming, even careful walking all share these features in some measure. The category is **mindful coupled movement with breath**. Find a flavor your nervous system will accept, and practice often.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Why Yoga Specifically?</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Three features that matter</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"940\" height=\"260\" rx=\"15\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.15)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Coupled Breath + Movement</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"310\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Every pose paired with breath.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"350\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Trains co-regulation of two systems</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"390\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">that trauma decouples.</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"470\" width=\"940\" height=\"260\" rx=\"15\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.15)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"530\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Interoceptive Demand</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"600\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Where is my foot? My breath?</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"640\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Direct insula training.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"680\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Small signals begin to land.</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"760\" width=\"940\" height=\"290\" rx=\"15\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"820\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Gradual Approach to the Body</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"890\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Carpet, props, predictable sequence.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"930\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Other forms work too: tai chi, dance,</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"970\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">qigong, swimming, mindful walking.</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Find a mindful movement flavor your system will accept."
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "quiz",
     "question": "According to Porges's polyvagal theory, what is the critical clinical risk of treating a patient in dorsal vagal shutdown as if they were in sympathetic activation?",
     "options": [
      {
       "text": "Interventions like vigorous breath, exposure, or high arousal can deepen the shutdown rather than reverse it",
       "correct": true
      },
      {
       "text": "It always speeds recovery",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "There is no clinical difference between the two states",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Patients prefer to stay in dorsal vagal state",
       "correct": false
      }
     ],
     "explanation": "Sympathetic states need discharge — walking, vigorous breath, movement — to return to ventral vagal connection. Dorsal vagal shutdown needs the opposite: gentle reactivation through voice, slow movement, soft contact. Forcing a shutdown patient through vigorous exposure typically deepens dissociation. Reading the state first prevents this common mismatch.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Quiz: State-Strategy Match</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mismatch deepens shutdown</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"940\" height=\"360\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.15)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The Risk</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"310\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Vigorous breath in shutdown</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"350\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">can push deeper into freeze.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"410\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Exposure too soon</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"445\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">re-traumatizes the patient.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"505\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\">Read the state before the technique.</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"580\" width=\"940\" height=\"280\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"640\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The Right Approach</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"710\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Shutdown → soft voice, slow movement.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"750\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Mobilization → discharge, then settle.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"810\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Connection → integrate and learn.</text></svg>",
      "caption": "State first; strategy second."
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "practice",
     "title": "Practice: A Five-Pose Trauma-Sensitive Sequence",
     "content": "You can try this in your living room, no class required. Move slowly. Stop any pose at any time. The point is interoception, not athleticism.\n\n**1. Mountain pose (1 min):** stand, feet hip-width, weight even. Notice the floor under your feet.\n\n**2. Gentle forward fold (1 min):** bend at hips, let head hang, knees soft. Notice your hamstrings, your breath.\n\n**3. Child's pose (2 min):** knees apart, big toes touch, hips back to heels, arms forward, forehead down. Notice your back rising and falling.\n\n**4. Supine knees-to-chest (1 min):** lie on your back, draw knees up, hands on shins. Notice low back releasing.\n\n**5. Savasana (3 min):** lie flat, arms slightly out, eyes closed or soft. Notice the whole body breathing.\n\nLog before and after: tension 1–10, breath rate, mood word. Eight minutes total. Do it three days this week and compare your notes.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Practice: 8-Minute Sequence</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Five trauma-sensitive poses</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"940\" height=\"480\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.15)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">1. Mountain pose — 1 min</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"305\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">2. Forward fold — 1 min</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"370\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">3. Child's pose — 2 min</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"435\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">4. Knees to chest — 1 min</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"500\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">5. Savasana — 3 min</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"600\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\">Stop any pose at any time. Point is interoception, not athleticism.</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"700\" width=\"940\" height=\"230\" rx=\"15\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"760\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Log Before and After</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"830\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Tension 1–10</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"870\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Breath rate, mood word</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Eight minutes, three days, compare your notes."
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "practice",
     "title": "Practice: A Polyvagal State Check",
     "content": "Set a phone alarm three times tomorrow. At each alarm, take 30 seconds to identify your state — green (ventral vagal, connected), yellow (sympathetic, mobilized), or red (dorsal vagal, shutdown).\n\nWrite the color, the time, and one phrase about what you notice in your body. Don't try to change anything. Just label.\n\nAfter three days, look at the pattern. Where in your day do you tend to drop into red? When do you spike to yellow? Does green show up at all? Often, simply naming the state begins to shift it — labeling is itself a mild ventral vagal act. And the data tells you which interventions to deploy when: yellow needs discharge (a walk, a vigorous breath), red needs gentle reactivation (humming, slow movement, soft contact), green wants to be protected (don't burn it on email). Carry the colors into lesson 10.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Practice: State Check</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Three alarms a day for three days</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"940\" height=\"320\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.15)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The Protocol</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"305\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Alarm → 30-second check.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"345\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Label green, yellow, or red.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"385\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Write color, time, body phrase.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"445\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\">Don't try to change anything yet.</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"540\" width=\"940\" height=\"340\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"600\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">What You'll Learn</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"660\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">When you tend to drop into red.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"700\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">When you spike to yellow.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"740\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Whether green appears at all.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"800\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Carry the data into lesson 10.</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Naming the state begins to shift the state."
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "quiz",
     "question": "Why does van der Kolk argue that body-based therapies are not 'alternative' or 'complementary' but essential for severe trauma?",
     "options": [
      {
       "text": "Trauma is encoded in body and brainstem; reaching these systems requires interventions language alone can't deliver",
       "correct": true
      },
      {
       "text": "Body therapies replace medication completely",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Only body therapies have any evidence base",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Talk therapy has been proven harmful",
       "correct": false
      }
     ],
     "explanation": "The injury sits partly in regions language can't reach: brainstem, limbic system, insula, autonomic nervous system. Body practices — yoga, somatic experiencing, polyvagal-informed work — act directly on these systems, restoring interoception and autonomic flexibility. They aren't optional add-ons; for many survivors they are the precondition that makes other treatments possible.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Quiz: Why Body First</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Not complementary; essential</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"940\" height=\"360\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">The Argument</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"310\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Injury sits in body and brainstem.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"350\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Language can't reach there.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"410\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Body practices act directly</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"445\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">on the dysregulated systems.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"505\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\">Precondition for other treatments to land.</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"580\" width=\"940\" height=\"280\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"640\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">In Practice</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"710\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Body work before, during, after</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"750\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">other trauma therapies.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"810\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Not bolt-on; foundation.</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Foundation, not afterthought."
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "quote",
     "title": "Coming Home to the Body",
     "content": "Van der Kolk's clearest one-line claim about body-based work:\n\n*'Trauma rips us out of our bodies. Healing is, in part, the work of coming home.'*\n\nThe insula stops mapping; the breath stops being felt; the body becomes territory we visit only when it hurts. The therapies in this lesson — yoga, somatic experiencing, polyvagal-informed practice — are guides for the journey back.\n\nThe next lesson takes a different route: into the brain itself, through neurofeedback — a technology that asks whether you can train your own brain waves to come home.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Coming Home to the Body</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#888\" text-anchor=\"middle\">A one-line claim</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"200\" width=\"940\" height=\"500\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.1)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"340\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">\"Trauma rips us out of our bodies.</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"425\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Healing is, in part, the work of</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"460\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">coming home.\"</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"600\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#888\">— van der Kolk on body-based therapy</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"740\" width=\"940\" height=\"310\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.15)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"800\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Coming next</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"870\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">Neurofeedback — training the</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"905\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">brain waves themselves to</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"965\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#fff\">come home.</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Body-based therapy as homecoming."
     }
    }
